1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,800
Okay, quick cold open.

2
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:10,883
If you have ever watched an AI agent write a huge diff, say done, and then leave you to discover the tests were imaginary, this is for you.

3
00:00:10,883 --> 00:00:13,954
Because the problem is not that AI is too slow.

4
00:00:13,954 --> 00:00:15,754
No.

5
00:00:15,754 --> 00:00:22,028
The problem is that AI is fast enough to create a convincing mess before your coffee cools down.

6
00:00:22,028 --> 00:00:27,380
Bubbles is the operating loop that asks the very annoying, very useful question: cool story, where is the proof?

7
00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:29,180
So what is Bubbles?

8
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:35,081
Bubbles is a spec-driven AI agent orchestration system for VS Code Copilot Chat.

9
00:00:35,081 --> 00:00:39,654
Translation: it gives Copilot a delivery crew and a clipboard.

10
00:00:39,654 --> 00:00:49,316
You get specialist agents for analysis, UX, design, planning, implementation, testing, docs, validation, audit, releases, and more.

11
00:00:49,316 --> 00:00:53,078
You get workflow modes for different kinds of work.

12
00:00:53,078 --> 00:00:58,831
You get gates that say, no, a confident paragraph is not the same as evidence.

13
00:00:58,831 --> 00:01:00,631
It is practical.

14
00:01:00,631 --> 00:01:02,431
It is strict.

15
00:01:02,431 --> 00:01:07,800
And, yes, it has a trailer park sense of humor because somebody had to make governance less beige.

16
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:09,600
Here is the shift.

17
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:11,400
AI made typing cheap.

18
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:13,200
That is excellent.

19
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:15,270
It did not make trust free.

20
00:01:15,270 --> 00:01:17,070
The bottleneck moved.

21
00:01:17,070 --> 00:01:19,830
Before, you spent time writing code.

22
00:01:19,830 --> 00:01:25,119
Now you spend time asking, did this actually satisfy the requirement?

23
00:01:25,119 --> 00:01:27,036
Did it break a user flow?

24
00:01:27,036 --> 00:01:30,639
Did the agent change the test to match the bug?

25
00:01:30,639 --> 00:01:34,702
Did the documentation drift into a decorative fossil?

26
00:01:34,702 --> 00:01:37,845
Bubbles is built for that new bottleneck.

27
00:01:37,845 --> 00:01:46,130
It turns the work into artifacts, tests, evidence, and certification so you are not just nodding at a cheerful final answer.

28
00:01:46,130 --> 00:01:47,930
Fake done is expensive.

29
00:01:47,930 --> 00:01:58,492
It feels fast because everybody gets to move on, and then two days later the same bug walks back in with a fake mustache and asks for database access.

30
00:01:58,492 --> 00:02:01,238
Bubbles is harsh about this on purpose.

31
00:02:01,238 --> 00:02:05,673
If a definition of done item is checked, it needs raw evidence.

32
00:02:05,673 --> 00:02:10,039
If a test claims to protect behavior, it must be able to fail.

33
00:02:10,039 --> 00:02:14,686
If a spec says done, the scopes underneath it need to be done too.

34
00:02:14,686 --> 00:02:16,939
This is not process for process.

35
00:02:16,939 --> 00:02:19,403
This is avoiding the fake done tax.

36
00:02:19,403 --> 00:02:22,180
And that tax has a terrible interest rate.

37
00:02:22,180 --> 00:02:25,052
The TPB-style mental model is simple.

38
00:02:25,052 --> 00:02:33,123
Picture a tiny operations office with too many clipboards and one person saying, hold on, who owns this?

39
00:02:33,123 --> 00:02:34,923
That is Bubbles.

40
00:02:34,923 --> 00:02:37,096
Analyst owns business truth.

41
00:02:37,096 --> 00:02:39,114
UX owns interaction flows.

42
00:02:39,114 --> 00:02:41,830
Design owns technical architecture.

43
00:02:41,830 --> 00:02:45,943
Plan owns scopes, scenarios, and definitions of done.

44
00:02:45,943 --> 00:02:47,743
Implement writes code.

45
00:02:47,743 --> 00:02:49,543
Test verifies.

46
00:02:49,543 --> 00:02:51,343
Docs publishes truth.

47
00:02:51,343 --> 00:02:53,516
Validate owns certification.

48
00:02:53,516 --> 00:02:55,316
Audit reviews.

49
00:02:55,316 --> 00:02:59,817
It means diagnostic agents do not casually rewrite design.

50
00:02:59,817 --> 00:03:02,844
Implementers do not certify themselves.

51
00:03:02,844 --> 00:03:06,960
And nobody gets to repaint the entire office because they found one crooked label.

52
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:11,127
Most of the time, you start with slash bubbles dot workflow.

53
00:03:11,127 --> 00:03:14,114
You do not need to memorize the whole crew.

54
00:03:14,114 --> 00:03:15,914
Just describe the work.

55
00:03:15,914 --> 00:03:17,714
Fix this regression.

56
00:03:17,714 --> 00:03:19,728
Improve this onboarding flow.

57
00:03:19,728 --> 00:03:21,534
Continue the current spec.

58
00:03:21,534 --> 00:03:23,756
Prepare the next release packet.

59
00:03:23,756 --> 00:03:32,299
Workflow resolves intent, picks the right owners, runs phases, enforces status ceilings, and routes rework when gates fail.

60
00:03:32,299 --> 00:03:35,841
The important part is that the work leaves a trail.

61
00:03:35,841 --> 00:03:40,633
So the next session does not start with, wait, were we touching auth?

62
00:03:40,633 --> 00:03:48,910
It starts with: here are the artifacts, here is the scope, here is the evidence, and here is what remains, which is much better.

63
00:03:48,910 --> 00:03:54,948
Actual developer problem number one: the UI calls an endpoint that does not exist.

64
00:03:54,948 --> 00:03:56,748
Or the method is wrong.

65
00:03:56,748 --> 00:03:58,956
Or the response shape changed.

66
00:03:58,956 --> 00:04:04,700
Or the test intercepted the request and gave everybody a little bedtime story.

67
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:11,768
Bubbles has vertical slice and integration completeness gates for exactly this kind of nonsense.

68
00:04:11,768 --> 00:04:15,008
Frontend calls need matching backend routes.

69
00:04:15,008 --> 00:04:19,057
New endpoints need consumers or external documentation.

70
00:04:19,057 --> 00:04:24,653
User-visible behavior needs scenarios and tests that exercise the real path.

71
00:04:24,653 --> 00:04:31,869
It is the difference between, the demo works on my laptop, and, the system path is actually wired.

72
00:04:31,869 --> 00:04:33,669
Tiny difference.

73
00:04:33,669 --> 00:04:34,560
Enormous Friday night.

74
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:41,000
Actual developer problem number two: the test passes because it carefully avoids the bug.

75
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:42,800
You have seen it.

76
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:44,754
If login redirects, return.

77
00:04:44,754 --> 00:04:48,083
If the layout is missing, skip that assertion.

78
00:04:48,083 --> 00:04:56,332
If every fixture has the field the old bug required, congratulations, the regression test is ceremonial furniture.

79
00:04:56,332 --> 00:04:59,661
Bubbles pushes tests toward adversarial proof.

80
00:04:59,661 --> 00:05:04,147
A bug fix needs an input that would fail if the bug came back.

81
00:05:04,147 --> 00:05:08,561
Required E2E tests must fail loudly when behavior is missing.

82
00:05:08,561 --> 00:05:14,350
Live-stack tests cannot quietly fake the backend and still call themselves live.

83
00:05:14,350 --> 00:05:18,050
That one rule alone saves a lot of very confident nonsense.

84
00:05:18,050 --> 00:05:22,272
Actual developer problem number three: context vanishes.

85
00:05:22,272 --> 00:05:24,072
The chat was long.

86
00:05:24,072 --> 00:05:25,956
The work was complicated.

87
00:05:25,956 --> 00:05:35,153
Then the session compacts, the thread gets fuzzy, and now everybody is guessing what the last agent meant by mostly fixed.

88
00:05:35,153 --> 00:05:37,716
Bubbles centers durable artifacts.

89
00:05:37,716 --> 00:05:43,898
Specs, designs, scopes, reports, user validation, and state files carry the truth.

90
00:05:43,898 --> 00:05:47,290
Recap and handoff help move between sessions.

91
00:05:47,290 --> 00:05:50,758
Workflow can continue from the artifact state.

92
00:05:50,758 --> 00:05:53,773
That is boring in the best possible way.

93
00:05:53,773 --> 00:05:55,573
Boring is underrated.

94
00:05:55,573 --> 00:06:00,190
Boring lets you ship without reconstructing the plot from tire tracks.

95
00:06:00,190 --> 00:06:03,362
Installation is intentionally not dramatic.

96
00:06:03,362 --> 00:06:06,682
You install the framework assets into a repo.

97
00:06:06,682 --> 00:06:15,313
Bootstrap sets up the local Bubbles layer: agents, prompts, instructions, skills, templates, and governance surfaces.

98
00:06:15,313 --> 00:06:18,485
Then start with slash bubbles dot workflow.

99
00:06:18,485 --> 00:06:20,285
Give it a real problem.

100
00:06:20,285 --> 00:06:23,088
Not a toy problem if you can avoid it.

101
00:06:23,088 --> 00:06:30,465
Pick the annoying bug, the drifted feature, the release packet that always becomes a scavenger hunt.

102
00:06:30,465 --> 00:06:33,489
The point is not to admire the framework.

103
00:06:33,489 --> 00:06:39,140
The point is to see whether the evidence trail makes the work easier to trust.

104
00:06:39,140 --> 00:06:41,539
For small jobs, use a specialist.

105
00:06:41,539 --> 00:06:44,955
If you want an idea pressure-tested, use grill.

106
00:06:44,955 --> 00:06:48,589
If you want engineering findings, use code-review.

107
00:06:48,589 --> 00:06:51,133
If you need docs aligned, use docs.

108
00:06:51,133 --> 00:06:54,476
If you need a clean status report, use status.

109
00:06:54,476 --> 00:06:57,384
If you need the story so far, use recap.

110
00:06:57,384 --> 00:06:59,184
This is important.

111
00:06:59,184 --> 00:07:02,382
Bubbles is not maximum process all the time.

112
00:07:02,382 --> 00:07:04,182
That would be awful.

113
00:07:04,182 --> 00:07:07,525
It is the right tool for the size of the work.

114
00:07:07,525 --> 00:07:09,924
Sometimes you need the full crew.

115
00:07:09,924 --> 00:07:14,610
Sometimes you need one person with a flashlight and a suspicious expression.

116
00:07:14,610 --> 00:07:19,269
For normal feature and bug work, workflow is the everyday workhorse.

117
00:07:19,269 --> 00:07:29,065
It can analyze the request, pick the next executable slice, route to owners, run phases, enforce gates, and packet rework when something fails.

118
00:07:29,065 --> 00:07:32,628
This is where Bubbles feels different from a prompt.

119
00:07:32,628 --> 00:07:35,574
You are not just asking, please be careful.

120
00:07:35,574 --> 00:07:43,863
You are running a workflow that understands artifact ownership, validation, test integrity, and certification boundaries.

121
00:07:43,863 --> 00:07:48,179
And when validate gets unimpressed, that is not a vibe problem.

122
00:07:48,179 --> 00:07:50,440
That is the system doing its job.

123
00:07:50,440 --> 00:07:54,132
For bigger outcomes, Bubbles has bigger controllers.

124
00:07:54,132 --> 00:07:56,901
Goal handles one outcome across phases.

125
00:07:56,901 --> 00:08:01,659
Sprint handles several goals with prioritization and a time budget.

126
00:08:01,659 --> 00:08:08,546
Releases handles phase packets, launch context, carry-forward, and the business side of shipping.

127
00:08:08,546 --> 00:08:15,859
This is useful when the work is too broad for one specialist and too important for a vague instruction.

128
00:08:15,859 --> 00:08:22,108
You give the coordinator the steering wheel, then make the workflow prove what happened.

129
00:08:22,108 --> 00:08:23,908
It is still agentic work.

130
00:08:23,908 --> 00:08:25,967
It just has lane markers now.

131
00:08:25,967 --> 00:08:27,767
Very fancy.

132
00:08:27,767 --> 00:08:27,930
Almost civilized.

133
00:08:27,930 --> 00:08:29,730
Picture one real bug.

134
00:08:29,730 --> 00:08:34,084
Checkout fails when availability changes during payment.

135
00:08:34,084 --> 00:08:44,348
Without structure, an agent patches a handler, adds a happy-path test, says done, and everybody hopes physics takes the weekend off.

136
00:08:44,348 --> 00:08:48,469
With Bubbles, analyst clarifies the business outcome.

137
00:08:48,469 --> 00:08:51,502
Design shapes the transaction boundary.

138
00:08:51,502 --> 00:08:55,156
Plan creates scenarios and definitions of done.

139
00:08:55,156 --> 00:08:56,956
Implement changes code.

140
00:08:56,956 --> 00:09:00,222
Test adds adversarial regression coverage.

141
00:09:00,222 --> 00:09:02,022
Chaos can poke timing.

142
00:09:02,022 --> 00:09:03,966
Validate checks evidence.

143
00:09:03,966 --> 00:09:05,988
Audit names residual risk.

144
00:09:05,988 --> 00:09:07,788
Same AI assistance.

145
00:09:07,788 --> 00:09:09,887
Much better operating loop.

146
00:09:09,887 --> 00:09:12,065
The difference is not drama.

147
00:09:12,065 --> 00:09:13,340
The difference is proof.

148
00:09:13,340 --> 00:09:16,802
The obvious worry is, does this slow me down?

149
00:09:16,802 --> 00:09:18,726
Sometimes it adds a step.

150
00:09:18,726 --> 00:09:21,034
Usually it removes five later.

151
00:09:21,034 --> 00:09:22,834
That is the trade.

152
00:09:22,834 --> 00:09:24,634
Bubbles reduces rework.

153
00:09:24,634 --> 00:09:26,434
It reduces mystery.

154
00:09:26,434 --> 00:09:34,897
It reduces the weird feeling where a lot of code changed, but nobody can name the user behavior that improved.

155
00:09:34,897 --> 00:09:37,282
It does not replace creativity.

156
00:09:37,282 --> 00:09:39,821
It protects it from cleanup debt.

157
00:09:39,821 --> 00:09:45,500
Because nothing kills momentum like spending Thursday proving Tuesday was imaginary.

158
00:09:45,500 --> 00:09:47,300
Here is the payoff.

159
00:09:47,300 --> 00:09:49,965
Bubbles does not make agents magic.

160
00:09:49,965 --> 00:09:51,944
It makes them accountable.

161
00:09:51,944 --> 00:09:53,744
That is better.

162
00:09:53,744 --> 00:09:55,544
Magic is hard to debug.

163
00:09:55,544 --> 00:09:57,828
Accountability has file paths.

164
00:09:57,828 --> 00:09:59,655
Intent becomes artifact.

165
00:09:59,655 --> 00:10:01,455
Artifact becomes scope.

166
00:10:01,455 --> 00:10:03,663
Scope becomes implementation.

167
00:10:03,663 --> 00:10:06,099
Implementation becomes evidence.

168
00:10:06,099 --> 00:10:07,899
Evidence becomes trust.

169
00:10:07,899 --> 00:10:17,780
And trust is what lets you give agents bigger work without handing them the keys to the whole park and hoping they remember where the brakes are.

170
00:10:17,780 --> 00:10:20,209
So try it on one painful workflow.

171
00:10:20,209 --> 00:10:22,009
Not your whole company.

172
00:10:22,009 --> 00:10:24,938
Not every repository you have ever loved.

173
00:10:24,938 --> 00:10:26,738
One real workflow.

174
00:10:26,738 --> 00:10:29,310
Pick the bug that keeps coming back.

175
00:10:29,310 --> 00:10:33,167
Pick the feature where specs and implementation drift.

176
00:10:33,167 --> 00:10:37,168
Pick the release packet that always becomes archaeology.

177
00:10:37,168 --> 00:10:43,669
Install Bubbles, bootstrap the repo, run slash bubbles dot workflow, and inspect the trail.

178
00:10:43,669 --> 00:10:50,884
If you want AI coding help that moves fast and still shows its work, Bubbles is worth a serious look.

179
00:10:50,884 --> 00:10:52,684
It is strict.

180
00:10:52,684 --> 00:10:54,484
It is useful.

181
00:10:54,484 --> 00:10:57,000
And it is just funny enough that the clipboard does not ruin the day.
